GitHub Copilot: In the context of programming and compiler options, "pedantic" refers to a mode that enforces strict adherence to the language standards and generates warnings or errors for code that does not conform to these standards. This can help catch potential issues and ensure that the code is portable and follows best practices.

For example, in GCC and Clang, the `-pedantic` flag is used to enable this strict mode. When this flag is set, the compiler will issue warnings or errors for any code that does not strictly conform to the C or C++ standards.

Here is an example of how the `-pedantic` flag is used in a `CMakeLists.txt` file:

```cmake
if(CMAKE_COMPILER_IS_GNUCXX OR CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES "Clang")
  add_compile_options(-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic)
endif()
```

In this example, the `-pedantic` flag is added to the compiler options along with `-Wall` (which enables all warnings) and `-Wextra` (which enables additional warnings). This combination helps ensure that the code is written to a high standard and is free of common issues.

